\subsection{Method \code{predict()}}{
Returns the exact optimal breakpoints, either under a linear penalty or for a
specified number of change-points.
\subsection{Usage}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}\preformatted{Dynp$predict(pen = 0, nBkps = NULL)}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\subsection{Arguments}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
\describe{
\item{\code{pen}}{Numeric. Penalty per change-point; the change-point count is chosen by minimising
\eqn{\text{cost} + \text{pen} \cdot k} over \eqn{k = 0, \dots, \text{nBkpsMax}} (same convention
as \code{PELT}/\code{binSeg}/\code{Window}). Ignored if \code{nBkps} is supplied. Default: \code{0}.}

\item{\code{nBkps}}{Integer. If supplied, takes precedence over \code{pen}: returns the exact optimal
segmentation for exactly \code{nBkps} change-points. Treated as an upper bound, not a strict
requirement, in the same spirit as \code{binSeg}/\code{Window}: if \code{nBkps} exceeds \code{nBkpsMax}, it is
capped to \code{nBkpsMax} and a message reports the shortfall. Unlike \code{binSeg}/\code{Window}, the result
is still exact for whatever count is actually used -- capping only ever happens because the DP
table wasn't built that far (a configuration choice via \verb{$nBkpsMax}), not because \code{Dynp} ran
out of candidates the way a greedy search can. If no valid segmentation exists at all for the
(possibly capped) count -- only possible when \code{jump} is large relative to \code{minSize} -- this
still errors, since there is no well-defined smaller-but-still-what-you-asked-for answer to
fall back to. Default: \code{NULL}.}
}
\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}
\subsection{Details}{
With \code{nBkps = k}, this returns the segmentation into exactly \code{k} change-points that globally
minimises the total cost -- exact, unlike \code{binSeg$predict()} for the same \code{k}. With \code{pen},
this instead minimises the penalised cost over every change-point count the DP table covers,
matching how \code{PELT}/\code{binSeg}/\code{Window} already select a count from a penalty.

Both the DP table (via \verb{$fit()}) and the traceback here only depend on \code{minSize}/\code{jump}
admissible positions, so \verb{$predict()} itself is cheap (\code{O(nBkps)}) regardless of which mode
is used -- the cost was already paid in \verb{$fit()}.

Temporary segment end-points are saved to \code{private$.tmpEndPoints} after \verb{$predict()}, enabling
users to call \verb{$plot()} without specifying endpoints manually.
}

\subsection{Returns}{
An integer vector of regime end-points. By design, the last element is the
number of observations.
}
}
