\subsection{Method \code{costPath()}}{
Returns the exact minimal cost for every change-point count the DP table covers.
\subsection{Usage}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}\preformatted{Dynp$costPath()}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\subsection{Details}{
This is the data behind the "elbow method" for choosing the number of change-points:
plot it (see \verb{$getHistory()}/\verb{$plotElbow()}) and look for where the marginal decrease in cost
flattens out. It comes for free out of \verb{$fit()} -- no extra computation is triggered here.
}

\subsection{Returns}{
A numeric vector of length \code{resolvedNBkpsMax + 1}: element \code{k+1} is the exact minimal
total cost of segmenting the series into exactly \code{k} change-points, for \verb{k = 0, ..., resolvedNBkpsMax}.
\code{Inf} at position \code{k+1} means no valid segmentation with exactly \code{k} change-points exists given
\code{minSize}/\code{jump} (only possible when \code{jump} is large relative to \code{minSize}).
}
}
