\subsection{Method \code{getHistory()}}{
Retrieves the exact minimal cost for every change-point count the DP table covers,
as a \code{data.frame} (same shape as \code{binSeg}/\code{Window}'s \verb{$getHistory()}, minus \code{added_bkp}).
\subsection{Usage}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}\preformatted{Dynp$getHistory()}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\subsection{Details}{
Unlike \code{binSeg}/\code{Window}, there is no \code{added_bkp} column here: \code{binSeg} and \code{Window}
each build a single \emph{nested} sequence of change-points, where every \code{k}'s answer is the
previous \code{k-1}'s answer plus one more point, so "the breakpoint added at this step" is
well-defined. \code{Dynp}'s per-\code{k} solutions are each independently exact and need not be nested at
all -- the optimal segmentation for \code{k} change-points can differ completely from the one for
\code{k-1}. Use \verb{$predict(nBkps = k)} to get the full breakpoint set for a given \code{k}.
}

\subsection{Returns}{
A \code{data.frame} with two columns:
\describe{
\item{\code{k}}{The number of change-points, from \code{0} to the resolved \code{nBkpsMax}.}
\item{\code{cost}}{The exact minimal total cost of segmenting the series into exactly \code{k}
change-points (see \verb{$costPath()}).}
}
}
}
